           from, and exists only to explain an absence. */
        return {
            compatible: ranked.filter(batch => _calNativeBatchCompatible(batch, mode)),
            incompatible: ranked.filter(batch => !_calNativeBatchCompatible(batch, mode)).slice(0, 3)
        };
    }
    /* The post-shape choice (owner ruling 2026-08-16: creation must ask).
       Mode changes re-render the picker because batch compatibility depends
       on the mode; the batch the user already chose survives the re-render
       when it is still offered. */
    /* Typing in the count must NOT re-render the dialog — a full re-render
       rebuilds the input and the caret jumps to the end mid-keystroke. So this
       updates state plus the one hint line, and nothing else. Mode changes DO
       re-render (the batch list depends on mode), which is why the clamp lives
       in _calNativePostCount rather than only here: switching to
       "Video + Thumbnail" with 80 posts staged must come back as 50. */
    /*
     * The number input's own spinner is two ~7px arrows stacked inside the
     * field: a hard target on a trackpad, invisible on touch, and the reason
     * the owner called it "this horrible up and down thing".
     *
     * The replacement is `sv-stepper`, the house primitive -- NOT a second
     * implementation of it. The first draft of this change hand-rolled 36px
     * buttons and its own min/max disabling, which review caught: AGENTS.md
     * says browser-native number spinners are unacceptable on branded surfaces
     * and to REUSE the documented SyncView controls, and `_svStepperHtml` had
     * already been carrying a 44px-target stepper with `_svStepNumber` for the
     * clamp and `_svSyncStepper` for the disabled ends. Writing it again meant
     * a control that was 36px on touch and a second copy of logic that can
     * drift from the original.
     *
     * The wiring uses `opts.onchange`, which the primitive ALREADY has --
     * `_svStepperHtml` is not modified by a single byte. It cannot be: the
     * leave-lifecycle evidence packet is fingerprinted over the PTO source
     * slice, this function is inside it, and any edit here stales a published
     * 101-screenshot packet whose only sanctioned repair is a human re-reviewing
     * all 101. An additive `oninput` option looked free and was not; see
     * test/leave-evidence-fingerprint-coupling.js, which caught it.
     *
     * `onchange` is enough because `_svStepNumber` dispatches a bubbling
     * `change` after it writes, so a button press reaches this setter by the
     * same path a typed value does. The one difference is that a TYPED value
     * commits on blur or Enter rather than per keystroke, so the sub-issue hint
     * settles a moment later when someone types instead of pressing. That is
     * the correct trade against forking a primitive.
